\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
General Equilibrium: Homework 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a pure exchange economy with 2 commodities and 2 agents 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 , 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Total resources are given by 
\begin_inset Formula $(w^{m},w^{b})$
\end_inset

.
 The first commodity is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

music
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the second commodity is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bread.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Each agent's utility funciton is given as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{1}(m_{1},b_{1})=m_{1}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{1}^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}-k_{1}\ \mbox{and}\ u_{2}(m_{2},b_{2})=m_{2}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{2}^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}-k_{2}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{2}$
\end_inset

 represents the consumption time of music by agents 1 and 2, respectively,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{2}$
\end_inset

 represent the quantity of bread consumed by agents 1 and 2, respectively,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset

 are given constants.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the set of Pareto efficient points?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose the initial endowment for the first agent is 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(\nicefrac{3}{2},\nicefrac{1}{2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(\nicefrac{1}{2},\nicefrac{3}{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Compute the competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Check whether or not the answer of part b are Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that each agent's utility function is changed as follows:
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{u}_{1}(m_{1},m_{2},b_{1})=m_{1}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{1}^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}-m_{2}\ \mbox{and}\ \hat{u}_{2}(m_{1}m_{2},b_{2})=m_{2}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{2}^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}-m_{1}
\]

\end_inset

One interpretation of the above utility function is that the consumption
 of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

music
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by yourself increases your satisfaction, but someone else's consumption
 of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

music
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 decreases your satisfaction.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the critical level of total music endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

 beyond which any balanced allocation is Pareto inefficient?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the set of interior Pareto efficient points? (You need to give the
 set of Pareto efficient allocations for two cases: 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}\leq\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}>\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

.) Compare this answer with part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(\nicefrac{3}{2},\nicefrac{1}{2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(\nicefrac{1}{2},\nicefrac{3}{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Compute the competitive equilibrium.
 Is this Pareto efficient?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An allocation is Pareto efficient if it is feasible and there is no other
 feasible allocation such that one person would be better off and all other
 persons are not worse off.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and quasi-concave, then a necessary and sufficient condition
 for the interior points to be Parteo optimal (PO) is that 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{m_{1},b_{1}}=MRS_{m_{2},b_{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 Let us find each agent's MRS.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
MRS_{m_{1},b_{1}} & = & \frac{\frac{3}{5}m_{1}^{-\nicefrac{2}{5}}b_{1}^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}}{\frac{2}{5}m_{1}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{1}^{-\nicefrac{3}{5}}}=\frac{3}{2}\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}\\
MRS_{m_{2},b_{2}} & = & \frac{\frac{3}{5}m_{2}^{-\nicefrac{2}{5}}b_{2}^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}}{\frac{2}{5}m_{2}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{2}^{-\nicefrac{3}{5}}}=\frac{3}{2}\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the set of interior PO allocations is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO_{a}=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2})\in X_{1}\times X_{2}:x_{1}+x_{2}=(w^{m},w^{b})^{\prime},\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}=\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Now, consider the case when 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1A}(0,b_{1})=-k_{1}=u_{1B}(0,0)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(b_{1}>0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $u_{2B}>u_{2A}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[(0,0),(w_{m},w_{b})\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[(w_{m},w_{b}),(0,0)\right]$
\end_inset

 are also Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\frac{p_{b}}{p_{m}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
 Also, since both agents have Cobb-Douglas utility functions, then their
 respective demand curves are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}(p) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{9}{10}+\frac{3}{10}p\\
\frac{3}{5p}+\frac{1}{5}
\end{array}\right)\\
x_{2}(p) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{3}{10}+\frac{9}{10}p\\
\frac{1}{5p}+\frac{3}{5}
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Also, by Walras' Law, it is enough to show only one market clearing for
 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider the market for music.
 By the market clearing condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{9}{10}+\frac{3}{10}p+\frac{3}{10}+\frac{9}{10}p & = & 2\\
p & = & \frac{2}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, a competitive equilibrium is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[x_{1}^{*}.x_{2}^{*},p^{*}]=\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
\nicefrac{11}{10}\\
\nicefrac{11}{10}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
\nicefrac{9}{10}\\
\nicefrac{9}{10}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
\nicefrac{2}{3}
\end{array}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since preferences are locally non-satiated, then by the First Fundamental
 Theorem of Welfare Economics, the competitive equilibrium allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d, Section 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}<\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}$
\end_inset

 for any allocations that satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}+m_{2}=w^{m}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}+b_{2}=w^{b}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $SMRS_{1}=SMRS_{2}$
\end_inset

, the social marginal rates of substitution must be negative.
 Hence, the allocation will not be Pareto efficient.
 By the SMRS equality condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}}{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial b_{1}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{1}}}{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial b_{2}}} & = & \frac{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}}{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial b_{2}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{2}}}{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial b_{1}}}\nonumber \\
\frac{3}{2}\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}-\frac{5}{2}m_{2}^{-\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{2}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}} & = & \frac{3}{2}\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}-\frac{5}{2}m_{1}^{-\nicefrac{3}{5}}b_{1}^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}\nonumber \\
\frac{3}{2}\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}+\frac{5}{2}\left(\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}\right)^{\nicefrac{3}{5}} & = & \frac{3}{2}\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}+\frac{5}{2}\left(\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}\right)^{\nicefrac{3}{5}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

and thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}=\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}^{m}\equiv m_{1}+m_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}+m_{2}=\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}+b_{2}=w^{b}$
\end_inset

 into the equation (2), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}} & = & \frac{(w^{b}-b_{1})}{(\bar{w}^{m}-m_{1})}\nonumber \\
\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}} & = & \frac{w^{b}}{\bar{w}^{m}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Then, by (2) and (3), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}} & = & \frac{9}{25}\left(\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}\right)^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}\left(\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}\right)^{\nicefrac{2}{5}}\\
 & = & \frac{9}{25}\left(\frac{w^{b}}{\bar{w}^{m}}\right)^{\nicefrac{4}{5}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}=1
\]

\end_inset

Setting joint marginal benefit equal to joint marginal cost, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}} & = & \frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{1}}\\
\frac{9}{25}\left(\frac{w^{b}}{\bar{w}^{m}}\right)^{\nicefrac{4}{5}} & = & 1\\
\bar{w}^{m} & = & \left(\frac{9}{25}\right)^{\nicefrac{5}{4}}w^{b}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}^{m}=\left(\frac{9}{25}\right)^{\nicefrac{5}{4}}w^{b}$
\end_inset

 is the critical point that makes 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}-\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}=0$
\end_inset

, or equivalently 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}}{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial b_{1}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{1}}}{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial b_{2}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}}{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial b_{2}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{2}}}{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial b_{1}}}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}^{m}>\left(\frac{9}{25}\right)^{\nicefrac{5}{4}}w^{b}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}-\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}<0$
\end_inset

, and any balanced allocation is Pareto inefficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d, Section 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Case:
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}\leq\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}<\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}-\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}>0$
\end_inset

, and by Proposition 11.2.1, no Pareto optimal allocation requires destruction.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}=\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

, then any allocation that satisfies the SMRS equality condition and the
 balanced conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}+m_{2}=w^{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}+b_{2}=w^{b}$
\end_inset

 also satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}\geq\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}$
\end_inset

, and thus it is a Pareto efficient allocation with no destruction.
 Therefore, the set of interior Pareto efficient points is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO_{d}=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2})\in X_{1}\times X_{2}:x_{1}+x_{2}=(w^{m},w^{b})^{\prime},\ \frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}=\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Note, this set is essentially equal to the set of Pareto optimal allocations
 given in part a, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $PO_{a}=PO_{d}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Case: 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}>\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}>\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial m_{1}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial m_{2}}-\frac{\partial u_{1}}{m_{2}}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{m_{1}}<0$
\end_inset

, and by Proposition 11.2.1, there is destruction in any Pareto efficient
 allocation.
 Therefore, the set of interior Pareto efficient points is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO_{d}=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2})\in X_{1}\times X_{2}:\frac{b_{1}}{m_{1}}=\frac{b_{2}}{m_{2}},b_{1}+b_{2}=w^{b},m_{1}+m_{2}=w^{m}-D=\bar{w}^{m}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where D is the amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}$
\end_inset

destroyed to achieve Pareto efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the set of Pareto optimal allocations has reduced compared to
 the set given in part a, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $PO_{d}\subseteq PO_{a}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d, Section 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(\nicefrac{3}{2},\nicefrac{1}{2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(\nicefrac{1}{2},\nicefrac{3}{2})$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}=2=w^{b}$
\end_inset

, and the competitive equilibrium will be the same as in part b.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{m}=2>\left(\frac{9}{25}\right)^{\nicefrac{5}{4}}(2)=\bar{w}^{m}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the competitive equilibrium allocation is not Pareto efficient unless
 some music is taken away from the total endowment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Suppose that at fixed input prices of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

, a firm produces output with the differentiable and stricly convex cost
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $c(q,h)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $q\geq0$
\end_inset

, is its output level (whose price is 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is the level of a negative externality generated by the firm.
 The externality affects a single consumer, whose derived utility funciton
 takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi(h)+w$
\end_inset

.
 The actions of the firm and consumer do not affect any market prices.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the first-order condition for the firm's choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the first-order conditions characterizing the Pareto optimal levels
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the government taxes the firm's output level.
 Show that this cannot restore efficiency.
 Show that a direct tax on the externality can restore efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show, however, that in the limiting case where 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is necessarily produced in fixed proportions with 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(q)=\alpha q$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

, a tax on the firm's output can restore efficiency.
 What is the efficiency-restoring tax?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Note*
The solution can be found in Chapter 11 of Mas-Colell.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The firm will choose its output level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 to maximize its profit function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{q,h}\ pq-c(q,h)
\]

\end_inset

Taking partial derivatives, we derive the first-order conditions for the
 firm's choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{q}(q,h) & = & p\\
c_{h}(q,h) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the Pareto optimal levels of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, we wish to maximize the utilitarian social welfare function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{q,h}\ pq-c(q,h)+\psi(h)+w
\]

\end_inset

Since utility and profit are quasi-linear, then the weights placed on the
 consumer and the producer are equal to 1.
 Taking partial derivatives, we derive the first-order conditions for the
 Pareto optimal levels of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{q}(q,h) & = & p\\
c_{h}(q,h) & = & \psi^{\prime}(h)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the set of Pareto optimal levels of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO=\left\{ (q,h)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}:p=c_{q}(q,h),c_{h}(q,h)=\psi^{\prime}(h)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, the amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 the firm chooses to produce is not Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 The government taxes the firm's output level
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The firm will choose its output level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 to maximize its profit function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{q,h}\ (p-t)q-c(q,h)
\]

\end_inset

Taking partial derivatives, we derive the first-order conditions for the
 firm's choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{q}(q,h) & = & p-t\\
c_{h}(q,h) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The first-order condition for 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is still the same as in part a.
 Thus, government taxes on the firm's output level will not restore efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 The government issues a direct tax on the externality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The firm will choose its output level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 to maximize its profit function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{q,h}\ pq-c(q,h)-ht
\]

\end_inset

Taking partial derivatives, we derive the first-order conditions for the
 firm's choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{q}(q,h) & = & p\\
-c_{h}(q,h) & = & t
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

From part b, we found that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{h}(q,h)=\psi^{\prime}(h)$
\end_inset

 is a necessary condition to achieve the Pareto optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, if 
\begin_inset Formula $t=-\psi^{\prime}(h)$
\end_inset

, then efficiency is restored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $h(q)=\alpha q$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

.
 To find the Pareto optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, we wish to maximize the new utilitarian social welfare function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{q}\ pq-c(q,\alpha q)+\psi(\alpha q)+w
\]

\end_inset

Since utility and profit are quasi-linear, then the weights placed on the
 consumer and the producer are equal to 1.
 Taking the derivative of the function, we derive the first-order condition
 for the Pareto optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p-c_{q}(q,\alpha q)-\alpha c_{h}(q,\alpha q)+\alpha\psi^{\prime}(\alpha q)=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose the government decides to levy a tax on the firm's output.
 So, the firm will choose its output level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 to maximize its profit function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{q}\ (p-t)q-c(q,\alpha q)
\]

\end_inset

Taking the derivative of the function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, we derive the first-order condition for the firm's choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p-t-c_{q}(q,\alpha q)-\alpha c_{h}(q,\alpha q) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In order to achieve the socially optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, we want to make the first-order condition of the firm the same as the
 first-order condition of the social welfare function.
 When this happens, the firm will internalize the externality and choose
 the socially optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the efficiency-restoring tax is 
\begin_inset Formula $t=-\alpha\psi^{\prime}(\alpha q)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Lake Ec can be freely accessed by fishermen.
 The cost of sending a boat out on the lake is 
\begin_inset Formula $r>0$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 boats are sent out onto the lake, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(b)$
\end_inset

 fish are caught in total (so each boat catches 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{f(b)}{b}$
\end_inset

 fish), where 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime}(b)>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime\prime}(b)<0$
\end_inset

 at all 
\begin_inset Formula $b\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 The price of fish is 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

, which is unaffected by the level of catch from Lake Ec.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Characterize the equilibrium number of boats that are sent out on the lake.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Characterize the optimal number of boats that should be sent out on the
 lake.
 Compare this with your answer in part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What pre-boat fishing tax would restore efficiency?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the lake is instead owned by a signle individual who can choose
 how many boats to send out.
 What level would this owner choose?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Note*
The solution can be found in Chapter 11 of Mas-Colell.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us consider the choice of an individual fisherman of whether or not
 to take his boat out to go fishing.
 The fisherman wishes to maximize his payoff function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x}\ x\left(p\frac{f(b)}{b}-r\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is the number of boats sent out onto the lake, but it is fixed for an individua
l fisherman.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 represents the fisherman's choice function and is defined as follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ p\frac{f(b)}{b}>r\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\frac{f(b)}{b}>r$
\end_inset

, then every fisherman will choose to go fishing, and eventually 
\begin_inset Formula $p\frac{f(b)}{b}-r<0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $p\frac{f(b)}{b}<r$
\end_inset

, then no fishermen will go onto the lake.
 Thus, equilibrium occurs when
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\frac{f(b)}{b}=r
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The socially optimal number of boats that should be sent out on the lake
 will maximize the collective payoffs of all the fishermen
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{b}\ pf(b)-rb
\]

\end_inset

Taking the derivative of the function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, we derive the first-order condition for the optimal number of boats
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
pf^{\prime}(b^{*})=r
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the number of boats that satisfies this first-order condition.
 In part a, the number of boats on the lake will be too large from a social
 point of view because an individual fisherman only takes account of his
 own payoff.
 He ignores the fact that as the number of boats sent out onto the lake
 increases beyond a critical level 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{b}$
\end_inset

, the amount of fish he catches will decrease due to decreasing marginal
 returns of 
\begin_inset Formula $f(b)$
\end_inset

.
 In part b, we find the optimal number of boats by maximizing the social
 profit of the fishermen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a pre-boat fishing tax is levied on the fishermen, then by equation (4),
 the new equilibrium condition becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
p\frac{f(b)}{b} & = & r+t\nonumber \\
r & = & p\frac{f(b)}{b}-t
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

So, by equations (5) and (6), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
pf^{\prime}(b^{*}) & = & p\frac{f(b)}{b}-t\nonumber \\
t & = & p\frac{f(b)}{b}-pf^{\prime}(b^{*})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Thus, the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 given by equation (7) is the pre-boat tax that would restore efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the lake is owned by a single individual, then his decision concerning
 the number of boats to send out mirrors the problem presented in part b.
 Thus, he will choose the socially optimal level of boats, 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{*}$
\end_inset

, to send out onto the lake.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let there be 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 individuals who consume one private good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and one public good 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 Each individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is endowed with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 units of private good.
 There are no initial endowments for the public good, but the public good
 can be produced form the private good, according to a production technology
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\frac{1}{q}v
\]

\end_inset

The utility function of individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

 which is continuously differentiable satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}>0\ \ \forall\ i=1,2,\ldots,n
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Define the Lindahl equilibrium and Pareto efficiency for this economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For interior Pareto efficiency, is it necessary that all individuals have
 the same marginal rate of substitution, i.e., that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{y_{1}}}{\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial x_{1}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{y_{2}}}{\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial x_{2}}}=\cdots=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{n}}{y_{n}}}{\frac{\partial u_{n}}{\partial x_{n}}}
\]

\end_inset

If yes, give proof.
 If not, give a counter example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose the utility functions are of the quasi-linear form 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}=x_{i}+v_{i}(y)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 stricly increasing, strictly concave, and continuously differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
At interior Pareto efficient solutions 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*},\ldots,x_{n}^{*},y)$
\end_inset

, does the optimal amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 of public good vary depending on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*},\ldots,x_{n}^{*})$
\end_inset

? Why or why not?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is the answer to the previous question different when quasi-linearity is
 not assumed? Why or why not?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2,w_{1}=5,w_{2}=4$
\end_inset

, the production function is 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{1}{2}v$
\end_inset

, and the utility functions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{1} & = & x_{1}+\alpha\ln y\\
u_{2} & = & x_{2}+\beta\ln y
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Find the Lindahl equilibrium for this economy.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(Lindahl Equilibrium, one private good and one public good) 
\series default
An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})\in\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 is a Lindahl equilibrium if it is feasible and there exists a personalized
 price vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, one for each individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
(i) 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}+q_{i}^{*}y^{*}\leq w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
(ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y)\succ_{i}(x_{i}^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}+q_{i}^{*}y>w_{i}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
(iii) 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}^{*}=q^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
We call 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*},q_{1}^{*},\ldots,q_{n}^{*})$
\end_inset

 a Lindahl Equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
Pareto efficient
\emph default
 for a public goods economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 if it is feasible and there is no other feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:(x_{i}^{\prime},y^{\prime})\succsim_{i}(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k:(x_{k}^{\prime},y^{\prime})\succ_{k}(x_{k},y)$
\end_inset

.
 The conditions for Pareto efficiency are given by the feasibility constraints
 and the Lindahl-Samuelson condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}} & = & \frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}\\
\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+v & \leq & \sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\\
y & = & f(v)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For interior Pareto efficiency, it is not necessary that all individuals
 have the same marginal rate of substitution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, let 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2,L=1,K=1$
\end_inset

.
 Let each agent have the following utility function, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}=x_{i}y$
\end_inset

, and let the production function for the public good be 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{v}{q}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\neq x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRS_{yx_{1}}=\frac{x_{1}}{y}\neq\frac{x_{2}}{y}=MRS_{yx_{2}}
\]

\end_inset

However, we can still have Pareto optimality.
 By the Lindahl-Samuelson condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{x_{1}}{y}+\frac{x_{2}}{y}=q
\]

\end_inset

and from the production function for 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}+x_{2}=v
\]

\end_inset

By the feasibility constraint, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}+x_{2}+v & = & w_{1}+w_{2}\\
v & = & \frac{w_{1}+w_{2}}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}+x_{2}=\frac{w_{1}+w_{2}}{2}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the allocation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*},y^{*}]=\left[\frac{3}{8}(w_{1}+w_{2}),\frac{1}{8}(w_{1}+w_{2}),\frac{w_{1}+w_{2}}{2q}\right]
\]

\end_inset

is Pareto optimal, but the marginal rate of substitution of the agents is
 not equal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c, Section 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the case of quasi-linear functions,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(x_{i},y)=x_{i}+v_{i}(y)
\]

\end_inset

the Lindahl-Samuelson condition becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}u_{i}^{\prime}(y)=\frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}\equiv c^{\prime}(y)
\]

\end_inset

and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is uniquely determined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c, Section 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The answer to the previous question will be different when quai-linearity
 is not assumed.
 In general, the marginal willingness-to-pay for a public good depends on
 the amount of private goods consumed, and therefore, the efficient level
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This result is characterized by the Lindahl-Samuelson condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}=\frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part c, Section 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a Lindahl equilibrium, each agent is assigned a specific 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

personalized price,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, for the public good.
 For simplicity, we normalize the price of the private good to one.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agent 1 wishes to maximize his utility
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{1}=x_{1}+\alpha\ln y
\]

\end_inset

subject to his budget constraint
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}+q_{1}y=5
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{1}{2}v$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=5-q_{1}y$
\end_inset

 into the utility function, we take the derivative of the function with
 respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 to derive the first-order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-q_{1}+\frac{\alpha}{y} & = & 0\\
y & = & \frac{\alpha}{q_{1}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, when 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha<5$
\end_inset

, the demand functions for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1} & = & 5-\alpha\\
y_{1} & = & \frac{\alpha}{q_{1}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Agent 2 wishes to maximize his utility
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{2}=x_{2}+\beta\ln y
\]

\end_inset

subject to his budget constraint
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{2}+q_{2}y=4
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{1}{2}v$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=4-q_{2}y$
\end_inset

 into the utility function, we take the derivative of the function with
 respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 to derive the first order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-q_{2}+\frac{\beta}{y} & = & 0\\
y & = & \frac{\beta}{q_{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, when 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta<4$
\end_inset

, the demand function for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{2} & = & 4-\beta\\
y_{2} & = & \frac{\beta}{q_{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=y_{2}=y^{*}$
\end_inset

 at the equilibrium, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
q_{1}y^{*} & = & \alpha\\
q_{2}y^{*} & = & \beta
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Taking summation we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
qy^{*}=\alpha+\beta
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q=q_{1}+q_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y^{*}=\frac{\alpha+\beta}{q}
\]

\end_inset

By equation (8) and (9), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
q_{1} & = & q\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}\\
q_{2} & = & q\frac{\beta}{\alpha+\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note, we have the case of a public economy with one private good and one
 public good where 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{1}{q}v=\frac{1}{2}v$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $q=2$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y^{*}=\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
q_{1} & = & 2\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}\\
q_{2} & = & 2\frac{\beta}{\alpha+\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, when 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq5$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}=\frac{5}{q_{1}}
\]

\end_inset

In addition, when 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq4$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=0$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{2}=\frac{4}{q_{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, the set of Lindahl equilibria for this economy is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\left[z_{1}^{*},z_{2}^{*},p^{*},q_{1}^{*},q_{2}^{*}\right]\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha<5,\ \beta<4$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
5-\alpha\\
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
4-\beta\\
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\end{array}\right),1,2\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta},2\frac{\beta}{\alpha+\beta}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq5,\ \beta<4$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{5+\beta}{2}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
4-\beta\\
\frac{5+\beta}{2}
\end{array}\right),1,2\frac{5}{5+\beta},2\frac{\beta}{5+\beta}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha<5,\ \beta\geq4$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
5-\alpha\\
\frac{\alpha+4}{2}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{\alpha+4}{2}
\end{array}\right),1,2\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+4},2\frac{4}{\alpha+4}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq5,\ \beta\geq4$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{9}{2}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{9}{2}
\end{array}\right),1,2\frac{5}{9},2\frac{4}{9}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

